Porto is smaller than Lisbon, steeper, and easier to hold in your head: the river is at the bottom, the cathedral is at the top, and everything you want is on the slope between them or on the far bank.

<Leg from="lisbon" to="porto" />

Three hours by train from Lisbon, most of it flat and agricultural. The trains are cheap and the good ones sell out; book the Alfa Pendular a few days ahead.

## The two banks

The Ribeira — the riverside on the Porto side — is where the photographs are taken, and where the restaurants charge for the view. Eat one street back. Then cross the lower deck of the Dom Luís bridge to Gaia, where the port wine lodges are, and do one tasting, not four: the second is the same as the first with less attention.

Walk back over the *upper* deck, at dusk if you can arrange it. It is the best free thing in the city.

## The bookshop, and the queue for it

Livraria Lello is a beautiful bookshop that has become a ticketed attraction because of a novel its author says she never visited it for. If you go, go at opening; if you do not, the São Bento station a few minutes away has twenty thousand tiles and no queue.

## The Douro by train

The Linha do Douro leaves from São Bento and, after an hour of suburbs, drops onto the north bank of the river and stays there. Get off at Pinhão — the station is tiled, the valley is terraced to the sky, and there is a boat that goes upstream for an hour and back. The last train back is early; check it before you have lunch, not after.

## Where this goes on the map

Porto is a base, not a route. Put the Douro day in as one stop — Pinhão — and let the itinerary hold Porto for the other days; a map of a return journey drawn as legs is a map of a scribble.
